# Round glmpair's one box term: the wall of GLM-5.3-Flash's two-row verify over one step on the same
# load, V2/S1, which decides whether the native MTP draft (k = 1, NextN layer 45) can win. One process
# gives both: generate_glm5next --time --pair times the plain greedy steps (`time step`, `smoke`),
# cuts back to the prompt's end, then runs the same tokens as verifies of two rows that keep both rows
# (`time pass kind=pair`), each checked against the plain run's tokens.
# Derivation [derived] (A6000, plan (a), prose ids): S1 = C1 + F + h = 47.7 ms (our plain 20.95 tok/s);
# C1 13.5-18.5 ms of card front, F 2.5-3.5 ms of host fixed cost, so h 25.7-31.7 ms of host expert
# bytes; the shadow Cx 3.8-5.5 ms sits under the host leg. The verify runs each row's layer one layer
# apart: per MoE layer the host serves 2 legs (1.35-1.66 ms) while the card runs 2 fronts and shadows
# (0.8-1.1 ms), so the wall is the host's, V2 = (1 + rho) h + 2F + edge: rho 0.85-1.0 is the second
# row's expert bytes that miss what the first row read (0.854 is the all-host k=2 union share; every
# row reads its own experts, the union is only what the cache keeps), edge 1.5 ms (the dense lead and
# the first front before the host starts, the last back and the heads after it). V2 = 54.1-71.9 ms:
# V2/S1 = 1.13-1.51, center 1.32. MTP breaks even at 1.70 (alpha1 0.753); beating llama.cpp #27754's MTP
# arm (23.29 tok/s) needs <= 1.53.
kind: baseline
question: what does the two-row verify cost over one plain step on the same load: does the host serve
  one row's layer under the card's other row, as the walk's order allows?
term: V2/S1 @ n=97, depth 512, A6000: the p50 of `time pass kind=pair` over the p50 of `time step`
  (smoke p50_ms), both from one generate_glm5next --time --pair process, plan (a), prose ids
unit: ratio
# Corrected 2026-10-01 (paper round glmpaper-verify, trace replay of 49,975 windows at n_l 67 on 39 layers):
# ~~1.13..1.51, center 1.32~~. rho is not the union share: row 1 shares 0.288 of its host bytes with row 0 and
# 0.119 on the same CCD, so rho 0.93-1.0 (0.88 tail); h is 29.6-31.4 ms (4,055 MB a row at 129-137 GB/s), not
# 25.7-31.7. V2/S1 = 1.34-1.52, center 1.44 [derived]; its dominant term is (1 + rho) h, ~88 % of V2.
predict: 1.34..1.52
condition: one lease, one process, 96 plain steps then 48 verifies of two rows at the same positions
  (the pair cut back to the prompt's end, its capture before the first pass); every pass's two tokens
  equal the plain run's (else the process ends in a named error, no row); no row carries [cpu-busy] or
  [other-busy]; the full output to a file first. Command (needs the runner's BLOOMERY_GEN_PAIR=1,
  which appends --pair to the ours arm, and generate_glm5next's records CAPTURE_PAIR and TIME_PASS):
  BLOOMERY_DECODE_N=97 BLOOMERY_AB_ROUNDS=1 BLOOMERY_BOX_ENV='BLOOMERY_LEASE_CARD=docs/cards/glmpair-probe.card
  BLOOMERY_DECODE_N=97 BLOOMERY_GEN_PAIR=1' just depth-gpu-glm5next 512 > <log> 2>&1
reason: the one term of the MTP plan the derivation cannot close (glmmtp design); why now: the verify
  exists, and the glmmtpload and glmmtp rounds are sized by it
decide-in: open glmmtpload then glmmtp as planned; write V2/S1 into the MTP speed ratio (1 + alpha) /
  (V2/S1 + D/S1) with the measured value
decide-below: the host reuses more of row 0's experts than rho 0.85 says (cache reuse): re-derive rho
  from the pass's host leg and raise the MTP prediction before glmmtp; still open glmmtpload
decide-above: the two rows are not overlapping as the walk orders them: take one nsys of the verify
  (the host legs and the card's fronts per layer) before any MTP round, and hold glmmtp
